  "command": "cat > /app/paper.txt << 'EOF'\n# Research Advances in Code Agents and Red-teaming\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe rapid advancement of artificial intelligence has led to the proliferation of code agents—autonomous systems capable of generating, debugging, and executing code to solve complex programming tasks. These agents, powered by large language models (LLMs), have transformed software development workflows by enabling automated code completion, repository-level code generation, and interactive problem-solving. However, their autonomous capabilities also introduce significant security and trustworthiness concerns. This paper explores recent advances in code agent architectures and the emerging field of red-teaming for evaluating their safety and reliability. We examine how code agents operate, their applications, and the vulnerabilities they expose. We further investigate red-teaming methodologies specifically designed to test code agents, including adversarial prompt injection, reward hacking, and automated vulnerability discovery. Our discussion highlights critical challenges in ensuring the trustworthiness of code agents and proposes directions for future research in this rapidly evolving domain.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nCode agents represent one of the most significant breakthroughs in AI-assisted software development. Unlike traditional code completion tools, code agents possess the ability to understand complex instructions, interact with external APIs, execute code in sandboxed environments, and iterate on solutions through a feedback loop. Systems such as Devin, OpenDevin, and various autonomous coding assistants have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in tasks ranging from bug fixing to full application development.\n\nThe power of code agents lies in their ability to:\n- Parse natural language requirements and convert them into executable code\n- Interact with file systems, databases, and external services\n- Execute and debug code iteratively\n- Reason about software architecture and design patterns\n\nHowever, these capabilities introduce unique security challenges. Code agents operate with elevated privileges in development environments, can execute arbitrary code, and may inadvertently or maliciously introduce vulnerabilities, data exfiltration mechanisms, or backdoors. The potential for misuse—whether intentional through adversarial manipulation or accidental through model limitations—demands rigorous evaluation frameworks.\n\nRed-teaming, the practice of systematically testing systems for vulnerabilities by adopting adversarial perspectives, has emerged as a critical methodology for assessing AI safety. Traditional red-teaming for AI systems has focused on content generation models (e.g., testing for toxic content, misinformation, or privacy violations). However, code agents require specialized red-teaming approaches that account for their unique operational characteristics: code execution capabilities, tool-use permissions, and the potential for indirect attacks through generated code.\n\nThis paper contributes to the growing body of research on code agent safety by:\n1. Surveying recent advances in code agent architectures and capabilities\n2. Examining specialized red-teaming frameworks designed for code agents\n3. Identifying key vulnerability classes specific to code agents\n4. Discussing open challenges and future research directions\n\n## 2. Related Work\n\n### 2.1 Code Agent Architectures\n\nRecent work has explored various architectures for building code agents. The foundational approach typically involves an LLM as the \"brain\" of the agent, which receives user instructions and generates code or tool-use commands. These commands are then executed in a sandboxed environment, and results are fed back to the model for further reasoning. This create-control-execute cycle enables agents to tackle multi-step programming tasks autonomously.\n\nKey developments include hierarchical planning approaches where agents decompose complex tasks into sub-tasks, tool-augmented agents that interface with package managers, version control systems, and testing frameworks, and multi-agent systems where specialized agents collaborate on software development tasks.\n\n### 2.2 Red-teaming for Code Agents\n\nA particularly important recent contribution to this domain is **RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents** (arXiv:2510.02609). This work introduces an automated framework for red-teaming code agents across diverse task domains. RedCodeAgent systematically generates adversarial prompts designed to elicit unsafe behavior from code agents, including:\n- Malicious code generation\n- Security vulnerability introduction\n- Privilege escalation attempts\n- Data exfiltration scenarios\n\nThe framework evaluates how susceptible different code agent architectures are to various attack vectors and provides a standardized benchmark for measuring code agent safety. This work establishes that code agents are vulnerable to a broader range of adversarial attacks than previously understood, necessitating specialized safety mechanisms.\n\n### 2.3 Trustworthiness Assessment of Large Language Models\n\nThe challenge of ensuring trustworthiness in AI systems has been extensively studied in the context of language models more broadly. **DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models** (arXiv:2306.11698) provides a systematic evaluation framework for assessing the trustworthiness of GPT models across multiple dimensions, including:\n- Bias and fairness\n- Toxicity and harmful content\n- Privacy violations\n- Factual accuracy\n- Instruction following\n\nWhile this work primarily focuses on text generation models, its methodological contributions inform the development of trustworthiness assessments for code agents. The framework's emphasis on comprehensive, multi-dimensional evaluation is particularly relevant for code agents, which must be assessed not only for the content they generate but also for the safety of the code they produce and the actions they perform.\n\nThe insights from DecodingTrust highlight that trustworthiness in AI systems is multifaceted and requires evaluation across diverse dimensions. For code agents, this extends to assessing the trustworthiness of generated code, the safety of tool-use behaviors, and the robustness of agent decision-making processes.\n\n### 2.4 Vulnerability Classes in Code Agents\n\nResearch has identified several classes of vulnerabilities specific to code agents:\n\n1. **Prompt Injection Attacks**: Adversarial inputs designed to manipulate agent behavior through crafted instructions or embedded code.\n\n2. **Tool Manipulation**: Attacks that exploit the agent's tool-use capabilities to perform unauthorized actions or access restricted resources.\n\n3. **Code Injection**: Generation of code containing vulnerabilities (e.g., SQL injection, XSS, buffer overflows) that can be exploited when deployed.\n\n4. **Reward Hacking**: Exploitation of the agent's optimization objectives to achieve desired behavior through unintended code patterns.\n\n5. **Information Disclosure**: Leakage of sensitive information through generated code, error messages, or tool outputs.\n\n## 3. Methodology for Code Agent Red-teaming\n\nEffective red-teaming of code agents requires addressing unique challenges:\n\n### 3.1 Automated Attack Generation\n\nManual red-teaming is insufficient given the large attack surface of code agents. Automated approaches must generate diverse adversarial prompts that test multiple vulnerability classes simultaneously. Recent frameworks employ:\n\n- **Genetic algorithms** to evolve effective attack prompts\n- **LLM-based attack generation** where one model generates attacks against another\n- **Template-based generation** combined with adversarial examples\n\n### 3.2 Sandbox Evaluation\n\nCode agents must be evaluated in controlled environments that:\n- Restrict network access or simulate realistic network conditions\n- Limit filesystem permissions to prevent data exfiltration\n- Monitor resource consumption to prevent denial-of-service behavior\n- Capture and analyze generated code for vulnerabilities\n\n### 3.3 Multi-dimensional Assessment\n\nBeyond detecting successful attacks, red-teaming should assess:\n- The agent's ability to recognize and resist malicious requests\n- Its tendency to refuse potentially unsafe requests\n- The quality and safety of any refusal responses\n\n## 4. Open Challenges\n\nSeveral challenges remain in advancing code agent safety:\n\n1. **Evaluation Standards**: Lack of standardized benchmarks for code agent safety makes it difficult to compare approaches and track progress.\n\n2. **Dynamic Environments**: Code agents often interact with evolving APIs and dependencies, making it challenging to maintain consistent safety guarantees.\n\n3. **Adversarial Robustness**: Many safety mechanisms can be circumvented through sophisticated adversarial attacks, creating an arms race between attackers and defenders.\n\n4. **Scalability**: As code agents become more capable, the attack surface expands exponentially, requiring scalable evaluation frameworks.\n\n5. **Multi-agent Systems**: Emerging multi-agent architectures introduce new attack surfaces where agents must be evaluated for both individual and collective behaviors.\n\n## 5. Conclusion\n\nCode agents represent a powerful new class of AI systems with transformative potential for software development. However, their autonomous capabilities and elevated privileges introduce significant security and trustworthiness concerns that demand rigorous attention. This paper has surveyed recent advances in code agent architectures and specialized red-teaming frameworks, highlighting the need for comprehensive safety evaluation methodologies.\n\nKey insights from recent work, including RedCodeAgent (arXiv:2510.02609) and DecodingTrust (arXiv:2306.11698), demonstrate that code agents are vulnerable to diverse attack vectors and require multi-dimensional trustworthiness assessments. Future research must focus on developing standardized evaluation benchmarks, improving adversarial robustness, and establishing safety guarantees for increasingly capable autonomous coding systems.\n\nAs code agents become more integrated into software development workflows, ensuring their trustworthiness will be critical for responsible deployment. The research community must continue developing robust red-teaming frameworks and safety mechanisms to mitigate risks while enabling the benefits of autonomous code generation and execution.\n\n## References\n\n[1] Chengquan Guo, Chulin Xie, Yu Yang et al. \"RedCodeAgent: Automatic Red-teaming Agent against Diverse Code Agents.\" *arXiv:2510.02609*, 2025.\n\n[2] Boxin Wang, Weixin Chen, Hengzhi Pei et al. \"DecodingTrust: A Comprehensive Assessment of Trustworthiness in GPT Models.\" *arXiv:2306.11698*, 2023.\nEOF"
}'}

